window.LLM_CARDS = [{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the shape of pred - target when pred is (4, 1) and target is (4,) ?","o":["(4,)","(4, 1)","(4, 4)","It raises a shape error"],"a":2,"w":"Shapes align from the right: (4, 1) and (1, 4) both stretch to (4, 4), with no error. The loss computed from it was 2.83 instead of 3.71."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"In bf16, what is 1.0 + 0.001?","o":["1.0","1.001","1.0009765625","inf"],"a":0,"w":"With 7 mantissa bits, the gap just above 1.0 is 0.0078, so adding 0.001 rounds back to 1.0. fp16 gives 1.0009765625."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A timer around c = a @ b on CUDA tensors reads 0.05 ms, but with torch.cuda.synchronize() before stopping it reads 11.2 ms. Why?","o":["The first run was cached","The call only queued the work; the timer stopped before the GPU finished","synchronize() slows the GPU down","The CPU did the first multiply"],"a":1,"w":"CUDA operations are asynchronous. Without a synchronise (or CUDA events), you measure only the launch."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"On this GPU, how much faster was a 4096 × 4096 matmul in bf16 than in plain fp32?","o":["About the same","About 1.4 times","About 2.8 times","About 130 times"],"a":2,"w":"88.8 against 31.6 TFLOPS. About 130 times is bf16 on the GPU against fp32 on the CPU; TF32 gave about 1.4 times."},{"n":0.1,"ti":"PyTorch Tensors on a GPU","f":"p1-pytorch-tensors.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why do training loops pin the memory of their input batches?","o":["Pinned memory uses fewer bytes","It stops batches being shuffled","The GPU cannot read pageable memory at all","Copies from pinned memory are faster and can overlap with compute"],"a":3,"w":"1 GiB copied in 18.6 ms from pinned memory (57.6 GB/s) against 48.7 ms from pageable memory (22 GB/s), and a non_blocking copy from pinned memory runs alongside compute."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why must a training loop call zero_grad() every step?","o":["It resets the learning rate","backward() adds to .grad , so old gradients would pile up","It frees the model's weights","It is only needed on the first step"],"a":1,"w":"Gradients accumulate by design. Without clearing them, each update uses the sum of all gradients so far; measured, the loss ended at 13,551 instead of 65."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The stability limit for plain gradient descent on the latency problem was 0.1015. What happened at a learning rate of 0.12?","o":["It converged faster than 0.1","It converged to a different answer","It stalled at the starting loss","The loss grew every step until it overflowed to infinity"],"a":3,"w":"Above the limit each step overshoots by more than the last: 6.8 million at step 10, then infinity."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An overfit-one-batch test gets stuck with the loss at exactly the variance of the targets. What is the most likely cause?","o":["The learning rate is too small","The model has too many parameters","The loss compares predictions with the wrong targets, for example through a broadcasting bug","The GPU ran out of memory"],"a":2,"w":"When every prediction is compared with every target, the best possible answer is the mean, and the loss is then the targets' variance: 0.6424 here."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did the forward pass with gradients use 704 MiB more memory but only 128 MiB without them?","o":["Autograd keeps intermediate activations for the backward pass, plus the weight gradients","Gradients are always stored in fp64","torch.no_grad() compresses the weights","The model is copied for the backward pass"],"a":0,"w":"Seven saved activations of 64 MiB, a 128 MiB fp32 copy of the output for the loss, and 128 MiB of weight gradients. Without gradients, each activation can be freed as soon as the next layer has used it."},{"n":0.2,"ti":"Autograd & the Training Loop","f":"p2-autograd-and-training-loops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does the cost of reverse-mode autograd compare with finite differences for a model with N parameters?","o":["Both need about N forward passes","Autograd needs about two forward passes' worth for all N; finite differences need about 2N forward passes","Finite differences are cheaper but less accurate","Autograd needs one backward pass per parameter"],"a":1,"w":"One backward pass yields every gradient at once, which is what makes training models with billions of parameters possible."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The naive softmax exp(z) / exp(z).sum() returned NaN on GPT-2's next-token logits even in fp32. Why?","o":["The largest logit overflowed to infinity","Every logit was around −124 or lower, so every exp() underflowed to 0 and the result was 0 / 0","The vocabulary is too large for fp32","GPT-2's logits contain NaN"],"a":1,"w":"exp(−124) ≈ 10⁻⁵⁴ is far below fp32's smallest value. Subtracting the max (−124.0) puts the largest term at exp(0) = 1."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Above roughly what value does exp(x) overflow to infinity in fp16?","o":["2","88.7","11.1","65,504"],"a":2,"w":"fp16's largest value is 65,504, and ln(65,504) ≈ 11.09. In fp32 and bf16 the limit is about 88.7."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"GPT-2 gave the correct next token a probability of 0.1592. What is that token's cross-entropy loss?","o":["About 1.84","About 0.16","About 0.84","About 6.28"],"a":0,"w":"−ln(0.1592) ≈ 1.84 nats, the value measured for \" 200\"."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A new language model with a 50,257-token vocabulary should have a loss close to what at step zero?","o":["0","1","3.9","10.8, which is ln(50,257)"],"a":3,"w":"Near-uniform predictions score ln(V) on every token; a freshly initialised head measured 11.02."},{"n":0.3,"ti":"Linear Layers, Softmax & Cross-Entropy","f":"p3-neural-network-building-blocks.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How does RMSNorm differ from LayerNorm?","o":["It normalises across the batch instead of across channels","It divides by the root mean square without subtracting the mean, and has no bias","It has no learned parameters at all","It is applied only to the output layer"],"a":1,"w":"Both act on each token's vector. RMSNorm keeps only the rescaling and a learned gain; on GPT-2's vector the mean was already near zero (0.03)."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Probability for Language Models","f":"p4-probability-for-language-models.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A model's mean negative log-likelihood on a text is 3.09 nats per token. What is its perplexity?","o":["3.09","About 9.5","About 22","About 1,230"],"a":2,"w":"e^3.09 ≈ 22, GPT-2's perplexity on the JSON body."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Probability for Language Models","f":"p4-probability-for-language-models.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why is a sequence's probability kept as a sum of log-probabilities?","o":["The product of many probabilities underflows to zero","Logs make the model more accurate","GPUs cannot multiply probabilities","It removes the need for softmax"],"a":0,"w":"For a 296-token passage the product was exactly zero after 33 tokens in fp32 and 210 in fp64; the log-likelihood was an ordinary −1,055.2."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Probability for Language Models","f":"p4-probability-for-language-models.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What is the entropy of a uniform distribution over GPT-2's 50,257 tokens?","o":["1 bit","10.8 bits","50,257 bits","About 15.6 bits"],"a":3,"w":"log₂ 50,257 ≈ 15.6 bits. 10.8 is the same quantity in nats (ln 50,257)."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Probability for Language Models","f":"p4-probability-for-language-models.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"After 10 samples, \" 200\" appeared once (10%), though its probability is 15.9%. What does that tell you?","o":["The sampler is biased","Nothing much: 10 draws are far too few, and 10,000 gave 16.1%","The model's probabilities are wrong","Sampling should use the top token only"],"a":1,"w":"A frequency from n draws has error of order 1/√n; with 10 draws, three of the six likeliest tokens never appeared at all."},{"n":0.4,"ti":"Probability for Language Models","f":"p4-probability-for-language-models.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why can a model score a whole training sequence in one forward pass but must generate one token at a time?","o":["Training uses a different model","Generation needs more memory","When scoring, every earlier token is already known; when generating, each next token depends on the one just chosen","Scoring only looks at the last token"],"a":2,"w":"A causal model predicts every position from its prefix in one pass; the results matched step-by-step scoring to within 5.4 × 10⁻⁵."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Text as Bytes: Unicode & UTF-8","f":"p5-text-bytes-and-unicode.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How many bytes does the euro sign € (U+20AC) take in UTF-8?","o":["1","2","3","4"],"a":2,"w":"E2 82 AC: a 3-byte sequence, as for Devanagari, Japanese and Chinese characters."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Text as Bytes: Unicode & UTF-8","f":"p5-text-bytes-and-unicode.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"For the string \"😀\", what do Python's len() and JavaScript's .length return?","o":["1 and 1","1 and 2","4 and 4","2 and 4"],"a":1,"w":"Python counts code points (1); JavaScript counts UTF-16 units, and characters above U+FFFF need two (a surrogate pair)."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Text as Bytes: Unicode & UTF-8","f":"p5-text-bytes-and-unicode.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"\"café\" typed as é (U+00E9) and as e + U+0301 compare unequal. What makes them equal?","o":["Lower-casing both","Encoding both as UTF-16","Stripping whitespace","Normalising both to NFC"],"a":3,"w":"NFC composes e + combining accent into U+00E9; GPT-2 had tokenised them as 3 and 4 tokens."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Text as Bytes: Unicode & UTF-8","f":"p5-text-bytes-and-unicode.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why did a Python function indented with spaces cost GPT-2 29 tokens but Qwen2.5 only 18?","o":["GPT-2's tokeniser has no tokens for runs of spaces, so each space is a token","GPT-2 cannot read Python","Qwen2.5 removes indentation","Spaces are 2 bytes in UTF-8"],"a":0,"w":"32 spaces were 32 GPT-2 tokens but 1 Qwen2.5 token; with tabs the same function was 20 and 16."},{"n":0.5,"ti":"Text as Bytes: Unicode & UTF-8","f":"p5-text-bytes-and-unicode.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"A zero-width space is inserted inside the word \"ignore\" in a user's message. What is the main risk?","o":["The model can no longer read the word","UTF-8 decoding fails","A filter matching the string \"ignore\" misses it, while the model still reads it","The message becomes longer than the context window"],"a":2,"w":"The word still looks the same and a model can read it, but it is now a different string, here 4 GPT-2 tokens instead of 1. Strip or flag format characters before filtering."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Matrix Multiplies & Counting FLOPs","f":"p6-matrix-multiplies-and-flops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"How many FLOPs does multiplying an (m × k) matrix by a (k × n) matrix take?","o":["m · k · n","2 · m · k · n","m · n","2 · (m · k + k · n)"],"a":1,"w":"Each of the m · n outputs is a length-k dot product: k multiplies and k additions."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Matrix Multiplies & Counting FLOPs","f":"p6-matrix-multiplies-and-flops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"For GPT-2 on 1,024 tokens, the rule 2 · N · T gave 254.9 GFLOPs but PyTorch counted 291.6. What did the rule leave out?","o":["The language-model head","The MLP layers","Attention's score and value multiplies, which grow with T²","The backward pass"],"a":2,"w":"Attention cost 38.7 GFLOPs at this length, and its share keeps growing with sequence length."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Matrix Multiplies & Counting FLOPs","f":"p6-matrix-multiplies-and-flops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"This GPU reads memory at about 830 GB/s and does 96.6 TFLOPS of bf16 compute. What does its ridge point of about 116 FLOPs per byte mean?","o":["Work doing fewer FLOPs per byte moved is limited by memory bandwidth, not arithmetic","It can do at most 116 FLOPs per second","Matrices larger than 116 rows are slow","bf16 values take 116 bytes"],"a":0,"w":"Below the ridge, the arithmetic units wait for data; only reading fewer bytes, or reusing each byte for more work, helps."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Matrix Multiplies & Counting FLOPs","f":"p6-matrix-multiplies-and-flops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"One row multiplied by a 4096 × 4096 bf16 weight ran at 0.43 TFLOPS. Why so slow?","o":["The GPU was not warmed up","bf16 is slow for single rows","The multiply was done on the CPU","It reads all 32 MiB of weights to do only 2 FLOPs per weight: memory-bound"],"a":3,"w":"Its 0.078 ms was spent almost entirely reading the weights. With 16,384 rows the same weights did 96.6 TFLOPS."},{"n":0.6,"ti":"Matrix Multiplies & Counting FLOPs","f":"p6-matrix-multiplies-and-flops.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What does cosine similarity remove compared with a raw dot product?","o":["The sign","The effect of the vectors' lengths, keeping only their directions","The need for a matrix multiply","Small values"],"a":1,"w":"It is the dot product divided by both lengths, so it ranges from −1 to 1 whatever the vectors' sizes."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"GPU Memory Budgets","f":"p7-gpu-memory-budgets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"About how much memory do a 7.6-billion-parameter model's weights need in bf16?","o":["About 7.6 GiB","About 14 GiB","About 28 GiB","About 3.5 GiB"],"a":1,"w":"2 bytes × 7.6 billion ≈ 15.2 GB = 14.2 GiB, almost all of a 16 GB card."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"GPU Memory Budgets","f":"p7-gpu-memory-budgets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"With fp32 master weights and AdamW, how many bytes per parameter does training need before activations?","o":["2","4","8","About 16"],"a":3,"w":"4 (weights) + 4 (gradients) + 8 (Adam's two moments). Measured after a step with gradients freed, 12 bytes per parameter remained."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"GPU Memory Budgets","f":"p7-gpu-memory-budgets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"GPT-2's training peak grew from 3.1 GiB at batch 1 to 13.1 GiB at batch 8. What grew?","o":["The weights","AdamW's state","Saved activations and the loss's temporary logits, which scale with batch × tokens","The CUDA context"],"a":2,"w":"About 1.43 MiB per token at the peak: 0.95 MiB of saved activations plus fp32 logits over a 50,257-token vocabulary."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"GPU Memory Budgets","f":"p7-gpu-memory-budgets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why does GPT-2 need more KV cache per token (36 KiB) than the much larger Qwen2.5-1.5B (28 KiB)?","o":["Qwen shares keys and values between groups of heads (grouped-query attention); GPT-2 keeps them for every head","GPT-2 stores its cache in fp32","Qwen does not use a KV cache","GPT-2 has more layers"],"a":0,"w":"GPT-2: 2 × 12 layers × 12 heads × 64 × 2 bytes. Qwen2.5-1.5B: 2 × 28 layers × 2 KV heads × 128 × 2 bytes."},{"n":0.7,"ti":"GPU Memory Budgets","f":"p7-gpu-memory-budgets.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"An out-of-memory error says \"Tried to allocate 896.00 MiB\". What does that number tell you?","o":["How much more memory the job needs in total","Only the size of the last request that failed; the total shortfall can be much larger","The size of the model's weights","How much memory the CUDA context uses"],"a":1,"w":"Work out the budget from its parts instead; the failing request is just where the sum crossed the limit."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Hugging Face & Model Files","f":"p8-hugging-face-and-model-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What do the first 8 bytes of a safetensors file contain?","o":["The length of the JSON header that follows","A magic number identifying the format","The number of tensors","A checksum of the weights"],"a":0,"w":"A little-endian 64-bit integer: 14,283 for GPT-2, followed by that many bytes of JSON and then the raw tensor data."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Hugging Face & Model Files","f":"p8-hugging-face-and-model-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"What happened when the canary checkpoint was loaded with torch.load(path, weights_only=False) ?","o":["It was refused with an UnpicklingError","It loaded the tensor and ignored the extra field","The function named in the file ran while it was being loaded: it printed its message","It was converted to safetensors"],"a":2,"w":"Unpickling calls whatever the file names. With weights_only=True (the default since PyTorch 2.6) it was refused instead."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Hugging Face & Model Files","f":"p8-hugging-face-and-model-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"You generate with Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct's weights but the base model's generation settings. What goes wrong?","o":["The model will not load","It may not stop at the end of its answer, because the base model ends with <|endoftext|> and the instruct model with <|im_end|>","The vocabulary sizes do not match","The weights load in fp32"],"a":1,"w":"Same vocabulary and template, different end-of-sequence token. Load the tokeniser and generation config from the same repository and revision as the weights."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Hugging Face & Model Files","f":"p8-hugging-face-and-model-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"Why pin a model to a commit hash instead of loading \"main\"?","o":["Hashes download faster","main is always the oldest version","It avoids the licence check","The repository can change later, silently changing results"],"a":3,"w":"GPT-2's repository has 26 commits; a pinned hash always loads the same files."},{"n":0.8,"ti":"Hugging Face & Model Files","f":"p8-hugging-face-and-model-files.html","lv":"Prerequisites","q":"The same from_pretrained(name) call loaded GPT-2 in fp32 and Qwen2.5-0.5B in bf16. Why?","o":["Qwen2.5 cannot run in fp32","GPT-2's weights are stored in fp16","Without a dtype argument, it follows each model's config, and only Qwen's sets bfloat16","bf16 is chosen for models over 400M parameters"],"a":2,"w":"GPT-2's config sets no dtype, so it loaded as stored (fp32); pass dtype explicitly so every model loads the way you intend."},{"n":1,"ti":"What a Language Model Is","f":"01-what-an-llm-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does a language model output at each step?","o":["The next word, chosen by the model","A complete answer to the prompt","A single score for the whole prompt","A probability for every token in its vocabulary as the next one"],"a":3,"w":"Choosing a token from that distribution is done by code around the model (greedy or sampling)."},{"n":1,"ti":"What a Language Model Is","f":"01-what-an-llm-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Given only \"What port does HTTPS use by default?\", what did the base model Qwen2.5-0.5B write?","o":["\"HTTPS uses port 443 by default.\" and then stopped","A multiple-choice exam question with the answer, then an unrelated question","Nothing: it predicted end-of-text immediately","\"The default port is 8080\""],"a":1,"w":"\"____ A. 443 B. 80 C. 23 D. 21 Answer: A\", then a question about cold fronts. A base model continues the kind of document the prompt resembles; \"8080\" was GPT-2's answer."},{"n":1,"ti":"What a Language Model Is","f":"01-what-an-llm-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why did the base model, given Qwen's chat template, answer correctly but then write stray tokens and new questions?","o":["It was never trained on that template, so it never learnt to end the turn","The template is broken","Greedy decoding always produces rubbish","The vocabulary was too small"],"a":0,"w":"Ending a turn is learnt during instruction tuning; the base model stopped by itself in only 3 of 20 answers."},{"n":1,"ti":"What a Language Model Is","f":"01-what-an-llm-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct answered 20 of 20 with its chat template. How many did it answer cleanly given the bare question?","o":["20","18","10","0"],"a":2,"w":"10 answered and 10 buried in exam-style continuations: without its template, an instruct model behaves much like a base model."},{"n":1,"ti":"What a Language Model Is","f":"01-what-an-llm-is.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What makes autoregressive generation stop?","o":["The model decides the answer is complete and returns it","The model predicts an end-of-sequence token, or the code reaches a length limit","The probability of every token falls below a threshold","The KV cache fills up"],"a":1,"w":"GPT-2 never predicted an end within 40 tokens; Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct stopped by itself in 18 of 20 answers."},{"n":2,"ti":"Tokenisation: Byte-Pair Encoding from Scratch","f":"02-tokenisation-bpe.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"At each step of BPE training, which pair is merged?","o":["The pair that makes the longest token","A random pair","The most frequent pair of adjacent tokens in the training text","The pair that appears in the most different languages"],"a":2,"w":"Greedy and frequency-based: merge 1 here was two spaces, from Python's indentation."},{"n":2,"ti":"Tokenisation: Byte-Pair Encoding from Scratch","f":"02-tokenisation-bpe.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why does a byte-level tokeniser never need an \"unknown\" token?","o":["Its base vocabulary contains all 256 bytes, and any text is a sequence of bytes","It has a token for every word in every language","It deletes characters it does not recognise","It falls back to character-level tokens from a dictionary"],"a":0,"w":"In the worst case a character becomes several one-byte tokens, as Hindi did here, but it is never lost."},{"n":2,"ti":"Tokenisation: Byte-Pair Encoding from Scratch","f":"02-tokenisation-bpe.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"A tokeniser trained only on English text was measured on Python. How did it do compared with one trained on English and Python?","o":["The same: tokenisers do not depend on their training text","Better, because English words appear in code","It could not encode Python at all","About half the compression: 1.68 against 3.39 bytes per token"],"a":3,"w":"It never learnt merges for indentation or common code pieces, so Python cost twice as many tokens."},{"n":2,"ti":"Tokenisation: Byte-Pair Encoding from Scratch","f":"02-tokenisation-bpe.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What is the main cost of a larger vocabulary?","o":["Longer sequences","More parameters in the embedding and output layers, and rarer tokens that get little training","It cannot encode emoji","It makes decoding lossy"],"a":1,"w":"Sequences get shorter, which is the benefit; every token costs a row in two large matrices (Chapter 3)."},{"n":2,"ti":"Tokenisation: Byte-Pair Encoding from Scratch","f":"02-tokenisation-bpe.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why are \"hello\" and \" hello\" different tokens in GPT-2?","o":["One of them is misspelt","GPT-2 lower-cases its input","Pre-tokenisation keeps a word's leading space attached to it, so the two strings are different byte sequences","Spaces are separate tokens in every tokeniser"],"a":2,"w":"They are ids 31373 and 23748: unrelated to the model until training teaches it otherwise."},{"n":3,"ti":"Embeddings & the Vocabulary Matrix","f":"03-embeddings.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does an embedding layer compute for a token id?","o":["A hash of the token's text","The row of the embedding matrix at that id, the same as a one-hot vector times the matrix","A full matrix multiply of the id by every row","The average of all rows"],"a":1,"w":"Row selection: 0.017 ms for 4,096 ids, against 9.18 ms for the one-hot multiply giving identical numbers."},{"n":3,"ti":"Embeddings & the Vocabulary Matrix","f":"03-embeddings.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Two random GPT-2 tokens have an average cosine similarity of 0.27. What does that mean when reading similarities?","o":["All tokens are synonyms","GPT-2's embeddings are broken","Cosine similarity cannot be used on embeddings","The rows share a common direction, so a similarity has to be compared with that baseline rather than with 0"],"a":3,"w":"The mean row has length 2.05 against a typical row's 3.95: every row leans the same way."},{"n":3,"ti":"Embeddings & the Vocabulary Matrix","f":"03-embeddings.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"For king − man + woman in GPT-2's embeddings, what was the nearest token when nothing was excluded?","o":["\" king\" itself","\" queen\"","\" woman\"","\" princess\""],"a":0,"w":"\" queen\" comes first only after excluding the three input words, as published demos usually do."},{"n":3,"ti":"Embeddings & the Vocabulary Matrix","f":"03-embeddings.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does weight tying do?","o":["Freezes the embeddings during training","Ties every layer to the same weights","Uses the input embedding matrix as the output (LM head) matrix too","Makes the vocabulary smaller"],"a":2,"w":"Scoring the final vector against the input matrix reproduced GPT-2's logits to within 3e-5."},{"n":3,"ti":"Embeddings & the Vocabulary Matrix","f":"03-embeddings.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why is the vocabulary's share of parameters smaller in Qwen2.5-7B (14%) than in Qwen2.5-0.5B (28%)?","o":["The 7B model has a much smaller vocabulary","The vocabulary matrix grows in proportion to the hidden size, while the rest of the model grows with its square","The 7B model has no embedding layer","The 7B model is tied and the 0.5B is not"],"a":1,"w":"Same 152k-row vocabulary; the 7B even untied it, with two 545 M matrices, and still spends only 14%."},{"n":4,"ti":"Self-Attention from Scratch","f":"04-self-attention.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"In self-attention, what decides how much position 7 takes from position 3?","o":["A fixed weight learnt for the distance 4","Position 3's value vector alone","The dot product of position 7's query with position 3's key, after softmax over all allowed positions","The number of tokens between them"],"a":2,"w":"Content decides: queries are matched against keys, and softmax turns the scores into weights."},{"n":4,"ti":"Self-Attention from Scratch","f":"04-self-attention.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why are scores divided by √d?","o":["Dot products of d-dimensional vectors spread out like √d, which saturates the softmax and shrinks its gradients","To make the weights sum to 1","To hide future positions","To save memory"],"a":0,"w":"At d = 64 the unscaled spread was 7.9, the top weight 0.79 and the gradient 2.6 times smaller."},{"n":4,"ti":"Self-Attention from Scratch","f":"04-self-attention.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"What does the causal mask do?","o":["Removes padding tokens","Sets weights below the diagonal to 0","Normalises each row of scores","Sets every score for a later position to −∞ before the softmax, so its weight is exactly 0"],"a":3,"w":"Changing the last token's key and value moved earlier outputs by 0.0 with the mask and 8.8 without it."},{"n":4,"ti":"Self-Attention from Scratch","f":"04-self-attention.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"From scratch, one layer of attention at 8,192 tokens needed 3.1 GB; the fused kernel 12 MB. Why?","o":["The fused kernel uses fewer heads","The from-scratch version stores T × T tables of scores and weights; the fused one works in tiles and stores only the output","The fused kernel approximates the softmax","The fused kernel uses int8"],"a":1,"w":"Same answer, to 2e-6; the memory grows with T² from scratch and with T fused."},{"n":4,"ti":"Self-Attention from Scratch","f":"04-self-attention.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"GPT-2's layer 5, head 5 at the second \" cat\" put 75% of its weight on \" sat\". What kind of head is that?","o":["A previous-token head","A first-token sink","An induction head: it finds the earlier copy of the current token and attends to what followed it","A head that attends to its own token"],"a":2,"w":"\" sat\" followed the first \" cat\", so the head predicts the pattern will repeat."},{"n":5,"ti":"Multi-Head Attention & Position","f":"05-multi-head-attention-and-positions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Going from 1 attention head of 768 dimensions to 12 heads of 64, what happens to the parameter count?","o":["It grows twelvefold","It stays the same: the projections are 768 × 768 either way","It shrinks twelvefold","It depends on the sequence length"],"a":1,"w":"2.36 million parameters whatever the split; only the number of T × T weight tables changes."},{"n":5,"ti":"Multi-Head Attention & Position","f":"05-multi-head-attention-and-positions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Without position information and without a mask, what does shuffling the input vectors do to attention's outputs?","o":["Changes them completely","Leaves them in the original order","Makes them all equal","Shuffles them the same way and leaves them otherwise unchanged"],"a":3,"w":"Measured difference 1.8 × 10⁻⁷: attention alone treats a sentence as a bag of words."},{"n":5,"ti":"Multi-Head Attention & Position","f":"05-multi-head-attention-and-positions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"With RoPE, the score between a query at position m and a key at position n depends on…","o":["Their contents and the difference m − n","Their contents and m + n","Only m","A learned table indexed by m and n"],"a":0,"w":"Positions (10, 3), (1010, 1003) and (30010, 30003) gave the same score, −3.686."},{"n":5,"ti":"Multi-Head Attention & Position","f":"05-multi-head-attention-and-positions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Why can GPT-2 not read a 2,000-token document in one pass?","o":["Its attention is too slow","Its tokeniser cannot encode it","Its learned position table has only 1,024 rows, so later positions have no vector","Its RoPE base is too small"],"a":2,"w":"A learned table is a hard limit; RoPE models can rescale their angles instead."},{"n":5,"ti":"Multi-Head Attention & Position","f":"05-multi-head-attention-and-positions.html","lv":"Beginner","q":"Removing GPT-2 head 0.10 added 2.19 to the loss; removing all of layer 0's heads added 0.66. What does that show?","o":["The measurement is broken","Ablations show what the rest of the network now relies on, not a simple per-part contribution","Layer 0 is unimportant","Heads in the same layer never interact"],"a":1,"w":"The other heads' outputs expect head 10's; without it they push the residual stream somewhere unfamiliar."}];
